Situation:
The Brigade Support Battalion sustains dispersed maneuver and support units across a contested area of operations. Supply Depot Alpha, Supply Depot Bravo, and Supply Depot Charlie hold limited daily stocks across multiple classes of supply. Ground lines of communication include open valley corridors exposed to indirect fire and IED threat, and narrow ridge routes with degraded trafficability. Air resupply is constrained by weather and enemy air defense; ground convoys are the primary distribution mode. Engineer route clearance and military police escort assets are limited and must be scheduled against competing movements and threat windows.

Mission:
BSB conducts ground distribution from the depots to supported units over the next few days to meet daily demand for required supply classes while minimizing total operational cost. Costs include per-unit transport, penalties for any unmet demand, per-vehicle deployment, and a fixed security risk cost to activate any route.

Execution/Concept:
- Flow and sourcing: Plan shipment quantities by depot, unit, supply class, and day. Shipments from any depot are authorized, subject to daily availability by class and location. Avoid overcommitting any single depot to preserve flexibility.
- Demand fulfillment and shortages: Aim to meet each unit’s daily requirements. Controlled shortfalls are permissible when operational risk or cost dictates; any unmet demand incurs a penalty reflecting readiness and mission impact.
- Vehicle loading and capacity: Load plans must respect vehicle payload/cube limits. Each supply class has a defined volume factor; the total loaded volume on any depot-to-unit movement cannot exceed the product of vehicle capacity and vehicles dispatched on that lane for that day.
- Route activation and security overhead: Vehicles may use only routes that are activated. Activating a route on a given day triggers a fixed Risk Cost once per route-day to account for escorts, route clearance, and overwatch; this cost applies regardless of how many vehicles traverse that route. Where feasible, consolidate shipments on fewer active routes to amortize the risk overhead while balancing exposure and transit time.
- Fleet management: The total number of vehicles dispatched across all lanes on any day cannot exceed the available fleet. Each vehicle used generates an operating cost; prioritize higher payoff movements and bundle loads to use the fewest vehicles consistent with mission risk.
- Cost trade space: Each depot-to-unit lane carries a per-unit transport cost; each unit-supply-day shortfall carries a penalty; each active route incurs a fixed risk cost; and each vehicle dispatched incurs an operating cost. The plan balances these factors to minimize total cost while meeting command priorities and preserving force protection.
- Mobility and threat constraints: Valley routes are vulnerable and may require additional standoff or night movement; ridge routes restrict convoy size and speed. Engineer and security assets limit simultaneous route activations. Weather and terrain may alter feasibility windows; reassess routes daily and adjust sourcing accordingly.
- Phasing: As depot inventories change over time, shift sourcing to maintain tempo. Preposition critical items when advantageous to reduce future risk and route activations.

